# November 24, 2026: Sioux Falls, South Dakota, a second pass over the city's old stone and brick buildings.
# The names came from a walk-through video of the city's old buildings (a tip only); every fact below comes from the
# National Register nomination texts on NPGallery, Wikipedia, SAH Archipedia, the managing bodies' own pages and
# local news pages, each opened when this was written. Most of these are working buildings seen from the street.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'siouxfalls_ixcd','label':'Sioux Falls, South Dakota: quartzite churches, schools and civic buildings','added':'2026-11-24 05:40'}
def NOM(n,label='National Register nomination'): return [label,'https://npgallery.nps.gov/NRHP/GetAsset/NRHP/%s_text'%n]
def SAH(n,label): return ['SAH Archipedia: '+label,'https://sah-archipedia.org/node/%s'%n]
DT='Downtown Sioux Falls: metered street parking and city ramps nearby. '
PLACES=[
N('carnegie-library-sioux-falls','Carnegie Free Public Library (Carnegie Town Hall), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.54572,-96.72937,'exact','view','walk','','1903–',
  'The city\'s public library, built in 1903 with a $25,000 Carnegie grant and designed by Joseph Schwartz. It is a Romanesque building of rough local quartzite with a Greek pediment and pilasters over the door, which the Register form calls the only stone building in the city with a Greek touch. The library moved out in 1972; an art association used it as a gallery until 1999, and since 2001 it has been the City Council\'s chambers.',
  'A one-storey quartzite block with a rounded apse at the back, a pediment and four pilasters round the door, and stone-mullioned windows with patterned upper panes. The 1973 form found golden-oak trim and a tiled fireplace inside and no major changes. Interior today not checked.',
  'The library that became the council chamber.',
  accn=DT+'235 West 10th Street. The City Council meets here; when the building opens to the public outside meetings was not checked, so look from the street.',
  links=[W('Carnegie Free Public Library (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),NOM('73001750')],brick='no'),
N('central-fire-station-sioux-falls','Central Fire Station (Station No. 1), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.54707,-96.7309,'exact','view','walk','','1913–',
  'Built in 1913 to a design by Joseph Schwarz for horse-drawn engines, with stalls on the main floor and the hayloft and oat bins above them. The city set up a paid fire department after the four-storey Cataract Hotel burned in 1900. Motor engines replaced the horses in 1917; the bell came out of the tower in 1957 because the tower had weakened. SAH Archipedia calls it one of the oldest working fire stations west of the Mississippi; it was fully restored in 2015.',
  'A two-storey station of rough Sioux quartzite with concrete quoins and trim, "Central Fire Station" cut in a concrete panel between two urns, three arched engine bays (now with modern doors) and a 65-foot hose tower on the north wing. Inside, SAH Archipedia lists three brass poles from the second-floor quarters. Interior not open to visitors as far as any source says.',
  'Built for horses in 1913 and still answering calls.',
  accn=DT+'100 South Minnesota Avenue. A working fire station of Sioux Falls Fire Rescue: look from the street and keep clear of the bay doors and the drive.',
  links=[W('Central Fire Station (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),NOM('80003730'),SAH('83125','Central Fire Station')],brick='no'),
N('federal-building-courthouse-sioux-falls','Federal Building and U.S. Courthouse (old post office), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.54322,-96.72623,'exact','view','walk','','1892–',
  'Built 1892 to 1895 to a design by Willoughby J. Edbrooke, the Treasury\'s architect, with the post office on the ground floor and the federal court above; Senator Richard Pettigrew\'s bill asked for native quartzite. It was lengthened and given a third floor in 1911 and a rear wing in 1931, both in the same stone. It is still the seat of the federal district court for South Dakota and was renamed the U.S. Courthouse at its centenary in 1995.',
  'A Romanesque block of rose-coloured quartzite from Jasper, Minnesota, with smooth quartzite trim that looks like terracotta, wide round arches, belt courses, a slate hipped roof and octagonal turrets at the ends; the main arch faces Phillips Avenue. Inside, the 1911 and 1931 work brought marble stairs and wainscot, terrazzo floors and brass elevator doors (Wikipedia, from the federal buildings agency). A 1995 county historical society marker is at the building.',
  'A frontier courthouse in Minnesota jasper.',
  accn=DT+'400 South Phillips Avenue. A working federal courthouse: anyone may look from the street; going in means the court\'s security screening, and the building is not run for sightseeing.',
  links=[W('Federal Building and United States Courthouse (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)')],brick='no'),
N('first-congregational-church-sioux-falls','First Congregational Church, Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','worship','standing',43.54396,-96.73044,'exact','view','walk','','1907–',
  'The congregation, the city\'s third oldest, met in a barracks for seven years and built a frame church nicknamed "God\'s Barn" in 1879. Joseph Schwarz designed the present quartzite church in 1907; the Register form ranks it among the finest Richardsonian Romanesque buildings in the state. An education wing was added in 1955.',
  'Rough quartzite walls, round arches and two front towers of unequal height, with a third tower-like entrance on the north side. The 1983 form describes an auditorium-style nave with the original stained glass in the narthex and balcony and 1960s glass in the big side windows. Wikipedia says a glass-and-panel front put on later was afterwards replaced by a copy of the 1907 stonework. Service times not checked.',
  'Schwarz\'s quartzite church that replaced "God\'s Barn".',
  accn=DT+'303 South Dakota Avenue. An active church: look from the street; no page of its own was read for service times.',
  links=[W('First Congregational Church (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),NOM('83003012')],brick='no'),
N('washington-high-school-pavilion-sioux-falls','Washington High School (Washington Pavilion), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.54399,-96.72905,'exact','open','walk','','1908–',
  'The city\'s high school, built in 1908 to a Neoclassical design by Joseph Schwartz. The school later moved to the northeast side of the city, and the old building was rebuilt as the Washington Pavilion of Arts and Science, opened in 1999: an art centre, concert hall, dome theatre and science museum. Listed on the National Register in 1986.',
  'A facade of large blocks of pinkish Sioux quartzite; Wikipedia notes a small part of the north wing in black stone once thought to be black quartzite, in fact diabase. Inside are the Kirby Science Discovery Center, the Visual Arts Center\'s galleries and two halls. The school interior was rebuilt for the Pavilion; original fabric inside not checked.',
  'A quartzite high school turned science museum.',
  accn=DT+'301 South Main Avenue. Run by the Washington Pavilion; admission to the science centre and galleries (free on the first Friday of the month, per its page). Hours from the Pavilion\'s own hours page, which says they are subject to change and to call guest services.',
  links=[W('Washington Pavilion of Arts and Science'),['Washington Pavilion: hours','https://washingtonpavilion.org/plan-your-visit/hours']],brick='no',
  hours='Visual Arts Center: Tuesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5; box office Monday to Saturday 10 to 5, Sunday noon to 5'),
N('cathedral-of-saint-joseph-sioux-falls','Cathedral of Saint Joseph, Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','worship','standing',43.55289,-96.735,'exact','open','walk','','1915–',
  'The seat of the Catholic Diocese of Sioux Falls, begun in 1915 to a design by Emmanuel Masqueray of New York on the site of the old brick St. Michael\'s cathedral; his assistant Edwin Lundie finished it after Masqueray died in 1917. Wartime shortages slowed the work; the first Mass was said on December 8, 1918 and it was dedicated on May 7, 1919. A fire in the lower church in 1942 was followed by redecoration, and a restoration in 2009 and 2010 took off paint that had covered much of the interior art.',
  'A twin-towered cathedral on the hill above downtown, the centrepiece of the Cathedral Historic District. Inside (Wikipedia): a marble high altar and canopy of 1946, French stencilled stained glass of 1947 and a rose window; a new pipe organ by Juget-Sinclair of Montreal was installed in 2025. A travel guide says volunteers lead weekday tours; that was not confirmed on the parish\'s own page.',
  'Masqueray\'s cathedral on the hill, finished in wartime.',
  accn='521 North Duluth Avenue, in the Cathedral Historic District; street parking round the block. An active cathedral: services are open to anyone. The parish\'s own site refused the request when this was written, so Mass times and tour hours were not checked.',
  links=[W('Cathedral of Saint Joseph (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),W('Cathedral Historic District (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('calvary-cathedral-sioux-falls','Calvary Cathedral (Episcopal), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','worship','standing',43.54217,-96.72784,'exact','open','walk','','1888–',
  'The parish began in 1872 and built the first church in Sioux Falls. The present Gothic Revival cathedral was paid for by John Jacob Astor III in memory of his wife Charlotte Augusta, at the request of Bishop William Hobart Hare: the cornerstone was laid on December 5, 1888, and it was consecrated in December 1889 as St. Augusta\'s, later taking back the Calvary name. It is the seat of the Episcopal Diocese of South Dakota.',
  'A Gothic Revival stone church with stained glass windows (Wikipedia shows one). Interior otherwise not checked.',
  'An Astor gift on the prairie.',
  accn=DT+'500 South Main Avenue. An active cathedral; services are open to anyone. Times from the Episcopal Church\'s parish listing for this address.',
  links=[W('Calvary Cathedral (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),['Episcopal Asset Map: Calvary Cathedral','https://www.episcopalassetmap.org/node/5081']],brick='?',
  hours='Sunday 8, 10 and 11:30 a.m.; Tuesday 5:30 p.m.; Wednesday 12:15 p.m.'),
N('all-saints-school-main-building-sioux-falls','All Saints School main building, Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.53735,-96.72942,'site','view','walk','','1884–',
  'A boarding school for the daughters of Episcopal missionaries, founded by Bishop William Hobart Hare, who lived in two rooms on the third floor and liked to watch the town from the cupola. The cornerstone of the main building is dated 1884. Listed on the National Register in 1973; at that time the building belonged to Calvary Cathedral\'s chapter. The campus later became a retirement community (search-result text quoting the All Saints historical marker; the marker page itself could not be opened).',
  'A four-storey "Prairie Gothic" building of irregular pink to rose local stone with wood trim, many dormers and chimneys, a bell tower and a cupola over the entrance. The 1973 form describes a one-and-a-half-storey chapel at the west end with signed Tiffany Studio windows and the original pipe organ. Interior today not checked.',
  'Bishop Hare\'s cupola and the Tiffany windows in the chapel.',
  accn='West 17th Street at Main Avenue, in the All Saints Historic District. Private: look from the street and do not go onto the grounds.',
  links=[W('All Saints School (Sioux Falls, South Dakota)'),NOM('73001748')],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('grand-lodge-masonic-library-sioux-falls','Grand Lodge and Library of the Free and Accepted Masons, Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.5429,-96.72872,'exact','view','walk','','1924–',
  'The state headquarters and library of the South Dakota Masons, decided on in 1923 and built from June 1924 into 1925. The 1975 Register form calls it the purest Neo-classical building in the city and says it held one of the largest Masonic libraries in the country, over 22,000 volumes, with a small museum upstairs. Its steel doors painted to look like wood and a nine-ton wire-reinforced glass floor were new in South Dakota then.',
  'A two-storey building with a limestone front of paired fluted Corinthian columns and pilasters, a cartouche and swags over the door, the lodge\'s name in the frieze and four vases on the attic; the sides and back are reddish-brown brick. Whether the library and museum still open to visitors was not checked.',
  'A glass floor and 22,000 books behind a Corinthian front.',
  accn=DT+'415 South Main Avenue. Look from the street; its present use and any visiting hours were not checked.',
  links=[W('Grand Lodge and Library of the Ancient Free and Accepted Masons'),NOM('76001750')],brick='some'),
N('phillips-block-sioux-falls','Phillips Block (Law Exchange Block), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.55107,-96.72866,'exact','view','walk','','1890–',
  'An office block across the street from the courthouse, begun for the attorney Andrew C. Phillips as the Law Exchange Block, with Wallace Dow as architect, and finished by his son. The 1977 Register form dates it 1891 to 1892 and says Phillips died in 1893; a 2026 local history piece says building began in 1890 and that he died in early 1891, before it was finished. It housed lawyers, then from 1908 the Queen City Fire Insurance Company, and from the 1950s the Soo Hotel; in 1977 it stood empty and new owners planned to restore it.',
  'Three storeys of reddish brick with decorative brickwork in the cornice and window panels, a Sioux quartzite foundation and basement, sandstone sills and arches, polished granite pillars and some cast iron on the front, and three big segmental windows at the corner. Interior not checked.',
  'A lawyers\' block across from the courthouse.',
  accn=DT+'335 North Main Avenue, opposite the Old Courthouse Museum. Private: look from the street.',
  links=[NOM('78002564'),['Dakota News Now: 335 North Main Avenue (2026)','https://www.dakotanewsnow.com/2026/09/17/throwback-thursday-335-north-main-avenue/']],conf='mixed',brick='yes'),
N('sioux-falls-coliseum','Sioux Falls Coliseum','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.55281,-96.72888,'site','view','walk','','1917–',
  'The city\'s auditorium, built in 1917, which held state basketball tournaments in its early years. It stands in the Old Courthouse and Warehouse Historic District. A county consultant recommended pulling it down for new county offices; in May 2026 the county commissioners put that off to look at other ways, and could still decide to raze it. It survived an earlier call to demolish it about 25 years before.',
  'A large auditorium of tan brick with white trim, a broad arched gable of tall windows and a row of pedimented windows over the entrance (as a photograph on Wikimedia Commons shows). Its future is undecided: check that it still stands before making a trip for it.',
  'A 1917 auditorium that keeps escaping the wrecking ball.',
  accn=DT+'West 5th Street at North Dakota Avenue. Owned by Minnehaha County and used for events; look from the street. Whether it can be visited outside events was not checked.',
  links=[['KELO: commissioners consider alternatives to tearing down the Coliseum (May 2026)','https://kelo.com/2026/05/05/917212/']],conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('columbus-college-va-sioux-falls','Columbus College buildings (Sioux Falls VA Medical Center)','Sioux Falls','sd','inst','standing',43.5311,-96.75506,'area','view','walk','','1921–',
  'The Catholic diocese bought 60 acres here in 1919 for Columbus College, a boys\' prep school, high school and college that had begun at Chamberlain in 1909. The main classroom building, a long V-shaped brick building by Edwin Lundie, opened in 1921; the college closed in 1929. The Veterans Administration took over the empty campus in 1944 and built the Royal C. Johnson Veterans Memorial Hospital beside it in 1946 to 1949, joined to the college by an enclosed walkway.',
  'The three-and-a-half-storey Tudor college building, with a Tudor-arched, crenellated entrance at the point of the V and dormers along the wings, and the brick hospital of 1949 with steep gables and a crenellated square tower on the rise, seen from much of the city (SAH Archipedia). Newer wings surround them.',
  'A failed Catholic college that became the veterans\' hospital.',
  accn='2501 West 22nd Street. A working VA medical centre: look from the public streets and the campus entrance at 22nd Street and Holly Avenue, where there are historical markers; patients and visitors only inside. The pin marks the campus, not one building.',
  links=[SAH('83119','Sioux Falls VA Medical Center')],brick='yes'),
N('augustana-old-main-sioux-falls','Old Main and East Hall, Augustana University, Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','inst','standing',43.52663,-96.73696,'site','view','walk','','1889–',
  'Built in 1889 for the Lutheran Normal School to a design by Joseph Schwarz. The school merged with Augustana College in 1918, which then moved to Sioux Falls; it became Augustana University in 2015. Beside Old Main stand Schwarz\'s Ladies\' Hall of 1904 to 1905 (East Hall since 1980) and the Tudor Gothic Administration Building of 1919 to 1920.',
  'A row of three on a stepped terrace along Summit Avenue: Old Main, three storeys of rough Sioux quartzite with a square tower and pyramid roof and deep round arches; East Hall in darker quartzite, joined to it by a small crenellated link; and the brick Administration Building trimmed in limestone, with turreted towers and a Tudor-arched door. Interiors not checked.',
  'The Lutheran Normal School\'s quartzite hall.',
  accn='2001 South Summit Avenue, on the north-east side of the Augustana University campus. Look from Summit Avenue; campus parking and visiting rules were not checked.',
  links=[SAH('83121','Old Main, East Hall, and Administration Building'),NOM('77001250')],brick='?'),
N('odd-fellows-block-sioux-falls','Pettigrew and Tate Building (Odd Fellows Block), Sioux Falls','Sioux Falls','sd','civic','standing',43.54657,-96.72861,'exact','view','walk','','1888–',
  'A commercial block built 1888 to 1889 for the real estate partners Richard F. Pettigrew, later the state\'s first U.S. senator, and Samuel L. Tate, whose firm ran several businesses from it. The upper floor was a meeting hall, and a plate on the front reads "Odd Fellows" and 1889. The 1986 Register form calls it one of the few quartzite commercial buildings left downtown.',
  'Three and a half storeys of Sioux quartzite with arched openings, pilasters, corbelling under the cornice, and two conical turrets joined by a balustrade either side of the Odd Fellows plate. The shopfronts have been changed and the stained glass transoms are gone; the sides and back are stuccoed. Interior not checked.',
  'Senator Pettigrew\'s office block with the Odd Fellows upstairs.',
  accn=DT+'121 to 123 South Main Avenue. Shops on the ground floor; look from the street.',
  links=[NOM('86002991')],brick='no'),
]
